Now that I'm done with
the recording formalities,

00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.122
this brings us to our
speaker this evening,

00:01:42.122 --> 00:01:44.699
Professor Gary Tomlinson, who is

00:01:44.699 --> 00:01:47.400
a true transdisciplinary and scholar.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.330
He serves as the current director

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.710
of the Whitney Humanities Center at Yale,

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.850
and Tomlinson is a musicologist
with a long commitment

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.580
to multidisciplinary
exploration and his teaching,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.980
lecturing, and scholarships
span and reflect

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.750
this broad intellectual diversity.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.380
Central among the the various
strong research interests

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.850
of his have been the traditions
of European classical music,

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.190
including the history of opera

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.780
and early modern musical
thought and practice,

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.070
but his work has always
opened up to broader visions

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.044
and perspectives the social context

00:02:23.044 --> 00:02:25.114
in which music making happens

00:02:25.114 --> 00:02:29.130
and he has produced many
wonderful essays and books

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.620
on music of the indigenous
American societies,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.550
jazz, cultural and anthropological theory,

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.423
and the philosophy of history.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.337
His recent book is titled

00:02:39.337 --> 00:02:41.547
"Culture and the Course
of Human Evolution"

00:02:41.547 --> 00:02:45.910
and I think in this book, he offers

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.937
a very intriguing and
compelling expanded view

00:02:48.937 --> 00:02:51.950
of our human origin story.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.100
So this latest project is part of work

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.984
that he has been doing over
the past couple of years

00:02:57.984 --> 00:03:02.320
intersecting with archeology
and evolutionary biology,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.690
looking at the role of musical capacities

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.990
in the emergence of modern humanity.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:11.840
He has several other books, which include,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.877
maybe I'll list a couple,
it's a long list, include

00:03:14.877 --> 00:03:16.597
"Monteverde and the End
of the Renaissance,"

00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:20.640
"Music in the Renaissance
Magic," and and so on.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.070
He's also looked at
indigenous voice in the era

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.590
of European contact and music
and historical critique.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.550
He is also, many of you, like I did,

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.220
might have known of
him, well I knew of him

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.730
well before I met him,

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.570
because of his music
appreciation book, text book,

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.890
called "Listen," which I encountered

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.810
when I was an undergraduate at MIT.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.670
It's now in its seventh edition.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.040
So Tomlinson was awarded a
BA from Dartmouth College

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.710
and a PhD from the University
of California at Berkeley.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:56.700
He arrived at Yale in
2010 after several years

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.400
as the distinguished Annenberg
Professor in the Humanities

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.767
at UPenn; he served as a Phi
Beta Kappa visiting scholar

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:06.800
and has had many many prizes from the

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.870
American Musicological
Society, the British Academy.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.560
He's a member of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.450
and recipient of Guggenheim
and the MacArthur Fellowships,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.020
so, it goes on and on, but
without much further ado,

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.850
because I am very impatient
to hear what he has to say,

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.537
his talk today is titled
"The Third Algorithm:

00:04:25.537 --> 00:04:27.190
"The Evolution of Human Culture."

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.346
Gary, it's a real
privilege to have you here.

00:04:29.346 --> 00:04:32.346
(audience clapping)

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.522
- Thank you, thank you all for being here,

00:04:39.522 --> 00:04:42.330
I truly thank you all for being here.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.310
It's a moist day and and many
of you were eager to go home

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.860
and find out what a complete
exoneration looks like

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.973
up close and personal,

00:04:51.964 --> 00:04:54.640
(audience laughing)
and I want to thank Priya

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.141
for the invitation to to
speak under the auspices

00:04:58.141 --> 00:05:01.270
of the wonderful Franke Program
in Science and Humanities

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.740
and of course I too, I
never tire of thanking

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.070
Rich and Barbara for all of
their extraordinary support,

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.481
and over the years, not
only for this program,

00:05:09.481 --> 00:05:11.380
but for many other programs at the Whitney

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.903
and many other programs
at Yale in general.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.733
So, "The Third Algorithm."

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:21.660
This is, as Priya has already
said, I somewhat immodestly

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.440
put up covers of a couple of recent books.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.580
This is an extension of the talk today.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.163
It is an extension of
these recent studies.

00:05:29.163 --> 00:05:31.360
The first of them, "A
Million Years of Music,"

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.270
was an attempt to understand
how it is that humans came,

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.510
what the evolutionary
dynamic was by which humans

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.724
came to be, music and creatures.

00:05:39.724 --> 00:05:43.290
There was a model of
cultural evolutionary dynamic

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.770
that was mooted there that
I wanted to explore more

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:46.947
and that's what happened in

00:05:46.947 --> 00:05:48.620
"Culture and the Course
of Human Evolution,"

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.870
and it is never, there's
no bottom to this question,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.210
I go on exploring it, and
some further exploration

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.940
is what I will be talking about today.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.040
The algorithm referred to in my title,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.890
I will get to the third,
I have to get to the first

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.480
and second first, however.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.000
The algorithm in my title,
what I mean by an algorithm

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.770
in this case is indeed
a set of conditions.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:11.550
I don't quite mean the
Google and Amazon things

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:12.690
that are controlling all our lives,

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.810
but rather a set of conditions

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.816
that set processes in motion, basically,

00:06:18.816 --> 00:06:21.460
and the first and foremost algorithm

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:24.420
in evolutionary thinking, as you all know,

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.792
is Darwin's natural selection.

00:06:26.792 --> 00:06:28.490
Here's the algorithm.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.450
If you have a group of organisms,
a population of organisms,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.040
that passes along traits
to successive generations,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.870
that is to say there is inheritance,

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.100
and you have, in that that inheritance,

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.720
there is some variation of those traits

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.620
from generation to generation,
and all of this is happening

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.974
in a landscape of not unlimited resources,

00:06:44.974 --> 00:06:46.560
there will be selection.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.840
The process is set in motion
by those conditions alone.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.440
I'm not going to read quotations

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.370
from a lot of my slides
today, but I do want to read

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.820
these wonderful words from
the beginning of chapter four

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.447
in "On the Origin of Species."

00:06:58.447 --> 00:07:01.217
"Can it be thought
improbable that variations

00:07:01.217 --> 00:07:02.677
"useful in some way to each being

00:07:02.677 --> 00:07:04.487
"in the great and complex battle of life

00:07:04.487 --> 00:07:06.127
"should sometimes occur in the course

00:07:06.127 --> 00:07:07.887
"of thousands of generations?

00:07:07.887 --> 00:07:10.637
"If such do occur, can we doubt,
remembering that many more

00:07:10.637 --> 00:07:13.017
"individuals are born
than can possibly survive,

00:07:13.017 --> 00:07:15.947
"that individuals having any
advantage, however slight,

00:07:15.947 --> 00:07:18.047
"over others would have the best chance

00:07:18.047 --> 00:07:20.146
"of surviving and procreating their kind.

00:07:20.146 --> 00:07:21.927
"On the other hand, we may feel sure

00:07:21.927 --> 00:07:23.887
"that any variation in
the least degree injurious

00:07:23.887 --> 00:07:25.887
"would be rigidly destroyed.

00:07:25.887 --> 00:07:27.847
"This preservation of
favorable variations,

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:29.617
"and the rejection of injurious variations

00:07:29.617 --> 00:07:32.592
"I call natural selection."

00:07:32.592 --> 00:07:37.390
Now, Darwin in his model
of natural selection

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:39.250
builds the question of resources into it.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.570
You can see that in this quotation,

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:43.362
in the reference to the
many more individuals

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.210
that are born than can possibly survive

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:47.210
or the complex battle of life,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.360
and indeed his model
for for thinking about

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.600
population dynamics in the first place,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.150
was Malthus's model, Thomas
Malthus from around 1800.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.431
The Malthusian model, as
most of you most of you

00:07:59.431 --> 00:08:02.390
are familiar with, is absolutely bound up

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.753
with the waxing and waning of resources.

00:08:04.753 --> 00:08:08.790
But this, when we take seriously
this question of resources,

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.100
we need to move beyond natural selection

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.250
to a second algorithm, a second algorithm

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:16.150
that has always been present.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.840
We can see it implicitly
in in Darwin's model,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.950
has always been present
in evolutionary thinking

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.960
since Darwin, but has in
recent decades burgeoned

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.000
into primary importance in
in evolutionary thinking.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.490
And this second algorithm

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:31.113
is the algorithm of niche construction.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:35.950
Niche construction, this
now is is a feedback loop,

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.600
a feedback cycle between the
environment and the organisms.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.200
The top of the feedback cycle here

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.860
is essentially Darwin's natural selection.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.680
The environment exerts, as
the metaphor usually has it,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.780
exerts a selective pressure
on organisms living in it.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.246
Some variants of those, or some variants

00:08:52.246 --> 00:08:55.693
are selected for, other
variants are selected against

00:08:55.693 --> 00:08:58.270
because of the nature of the
affordances and constraints

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.903
that the environment offers.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:03.150
The other side of this
feedback loop, however,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.280
is what niche construction
wants to focus on,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.320
and that is the fact that organisms

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.470
change their environments
in living in them,

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.930
that there are no organisms
anywhere in the history of life,

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.830
here or anywhere else,
wherever life may be,

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.510
that could possibly be so light-footed

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:17.670
that they would have no impact whatsoever

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:19.100
on their environments.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.460
If they do have impacts
on their environments,

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.110
however, we need to take account of that

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.600
in our model of natural selection,

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.000
because the selective
gradient, the selective terrain

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.970
of the environment is not
only changing the genomes

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.510
of these of these organisms
through many many generations

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.000
but the organisms in their behaviors,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.690
in their life ways, are
changing the environment.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:42.083
So the environment is changing
the organisms and vice versa.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.580
Now if we were to leap ahead to talk

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:48.590
about cultural organisms,
cultural animals, that is,

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:50.870
animals that that have cultural behaviors,

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.890
we would immediately understand
that in order to think

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.630
about niche construction
of cultural animals,

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.740
we're gonna have to think
about the role of culture

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.890
in changing the environment, right?

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.150
Culture is, and the cultures of animals

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:04.030
are not simply neutral, they are part

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.030
of their niche construction,
so the question

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.500
of how these cultures
change their their niches,

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.170
change the nature of the construction,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.643
is one that we will have to
build into our feedback cycle

00:10:13.643 --> 00:10:15.363
of niche construction.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.430
Now I've gotten ahead of myself,
because I haven't yet said

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:23.590
what I think of what a culture might be.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.406
Here's a definition of culture from,

00:10:25.406 --> 00:10:28.910
that evolutionists have taken
up for about the last 30 years

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.447
or so in trying to understand
the dynamics of culture

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.833
as it plays a part in
evolutionary process.

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:41.637
There it is, Pete Richerson
and Robert Boyd,

00:10:41.637 --> 00:10:44.097
"information learned and
transmitted from one generation

00:10:44.097 --> 00:10:47.010
"to another through teaching
imitation and similar means."

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.110
The crucial elements of this

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.430
are the learning of information,

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:50.670
the transmitting of information

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.680
through some sort of imitative
or pedagogical means.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.000
Now this is a hugely
broad view of culture.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.300
It's a view of culture that
is certainly broad enough

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.930
to open out our notion of cultural animals

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.500
well beyond Homo sapiens
in the world today.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.600
We can think of songbird cultures.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.250
We can think of humpback whale cultures.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.320
We can think of sperm
whale cultures, by the way,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.930
then the this is only
recently really made clear,

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:21.149
but the click codes by
which, that are learned

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:23.600
and transmitted from
generation to generation

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:26.460
are rather complex codes by which whales

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.550
not only identify their pods,

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:31.509
but they also identify
individuals within their pods.

00:11:31.509 --> 00:11:33.760
Whale cultures, in other
words, we can think of,

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.720
of course, a number of a
number of non-human primates

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.780
in the world today that have cultures

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:41.150
according to this broad definition,

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:44.290
chimpanzees, bonobos, maybe gorillas,

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:47.773
some other monkeys, even,
beyond beyond the great apes.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:52.620
So this a broad view of
culture is the first point.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.636
The second point before
I move on from this

00:11:54.636 --> 00:11:56.890
is that we need to think
a little bit more broadly

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.090
about the information
that is at stake here.

00:12:00.728 --> 00:12:03.900
We can think first that
there is a particular kind

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.707
of information that I want to get to

00:12:05.707 --> 00:12:08.560
and that is the information
that is embodied in culture,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.593
the information that is learned
and transmitted in culture.

00:12:13.858 --> 00:12:17.140
It plays a role and it is an outgrowth

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:19.810
of a much much broader
kind of information,

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:22.620
a much more broader
informational foundation.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.771
This broad informational foundation is,

00:12:24.771 --> 00:12:27.624
we can think of as
operative correspondences

00:12:27.624 --> 00:12:30.050
that are evolved into living things.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.070
Think of the relationship
between, the correspondences

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:36.750
between, a messenger RNA
molecule and a DNA molecule,

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.520
or the correspondences between
a messenger RNA molecule

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.410
with its codons and the
amino acids that bond

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:45.990
to those codons making up
polypeptides, proteins finally.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.368
These are these are all
informational processes

00:12:49.368 --> 00:12:51.960
in any useful definition of information

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.760
that has come along since the 1940s,

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.373
these would be considered
informational processes.

00:12:57.263 --> 00:12:59.700
But the information and
culture is of a different sort.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.603
The information and culture
is is information about.

00:13:02.603 --> 00:13:04.840
It is information about the niche.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:07.195
It is information about
the sociality of animals

00:13:07.195 --> 00:13:10.015
on the niche; it is the information

00:13:10.015 --> 00:13:13.490
about how it is that they move forward

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.382
in constructing their niche.

00:13:17.382 --> 00:13:19.049
The question then comes to be,

00:13:19.049 --> 00:13:22.010
how did this difference in
kinds of information come along?

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:24.163
How did aboutness arise?

00:13:25.232 --> 00:13:27.190
And that takes us to the third algorithm.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.920
The third algorithm is
sign-making, or semiosis.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:34.663
Semiosis is a special animal
interaction with the world,

00:13:34.663 --> 00:13:36.879
and it creates a very distinctive kind

00:13:36.879 --> 00:13:41.310
of information processing
in its complexity.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.813
And I've laid out a little chart here

00:13:43.813 --> 00:13:48.420
that gives you a starting point,
as I see it, for semiosis.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:50.780
Again this is the view
of semiosis not as doubt

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.620
that is going to be, like
culture, is going to be

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:54.960
broadly dispersed across
many many animals.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.210
Many more animals, in fact, are semiotic

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:01.013
than finally are cultural
animals in this view.

00:14:01.013 --> 00:14:02.220
Where do we start?

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.130
We start with the notion
of an attentive animal,

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.999
the notion of an animal that
can pay attention to things.

00:14:06.999 --> 00:14:08.413
This is not true of all animals.

00:14:08.413 --> 00:14:10.490
It's certainly not true
of plants, microbes,

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:13.834
and other, and molecules themselves,

00:14:13.834 --> 00:14:16.240
but the notion of attention is,

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.393
has been much researched recently

00:14:18.393 --> 00:14:20.727
by people from many many different fields.

00:14:20.727 --> 00:14:23.960
They take in the notion
of top-down processing

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:27.160
from complex central nervous functions,

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.990
such as memory, learning,
even rudimentary memory

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:33.760
and rudimentary learning,
they take into account also

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.979
the filtering of the
sensory stimuli that come

00:14:36.979 --> 00:14:40.312
to the animal from the outside world.

00:14:40.312 --> 00:14:43.280
They take all of those
things into account,

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.083
but what all of these views of semiosis,

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:50.673
all of these views of attention agree on,

00:14:50.673 --> 00:14:52.680
whatever their other differences,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.973
is that attention is first
of all a capacity to winnow

00:14:55.973 --> 00:15:00.973
or to focus or to, well,
to focus on certain stimuli

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.630
from among all of those
stimuli that are impinging

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:06.706
on the animal at any at any given moment.

00:15:06.706 --> 00:15:09.670
This notion of a winnowing
or a focusing is something

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.560
that then enables animals
to bring into salience

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:15.740
certain stimuli, while not
attending is quite so much

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:17.020
to other stimuli, right?

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.783
And not only that, because
it brings them into salience,

00:15:19.783 --> 00:15:23.830
in conjunction with these
top-down neural systems,

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.620
it enables the easier
memorization of them.

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.620
It enables learning about the stimuli.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:30.230
It enables many things.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:31.827
But one thing is, there's one thing

00:15:31.827 --> 00:15:36.000
that these attention researchers
don't quite pick out,

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.800
pick out as often as I think they might,

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.910
and that is the recursive
quality to attention.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.639
If you have an animal
that is able to winnow

00:15:42.639 --> 00:15:45.000
from among all the
stimuli impinging on it,

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.290
just a set of stimuli and
pay attention to those,

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.767
well we can recursively
see that that animal,

00:15:50.767 --> 00:15:53.867
given whatever the limit, within
the limits of its sensorium

00:15:53.867 --> 00:15:55.610
and and its cognitive patterns,

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:58.080
that animal might be
able to winnow further

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.660
and pay attention to a fewer,
a smaller number of stimuli

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:03.070
within the group of stimuli

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:05.240
that it might otherwise
have paid attention to.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:08.000
This opens up something that
is to me immensely important.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.549
It opens up the possibility of
attending not only to things,

00:16:12.549 --> 00:16:15.880
as in their wholes, but
attending to aspects or qualities

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:19.430
or traits of things in
very particular ways,

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:21.610
and this opens up the possibility, then,

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.410
of a part for whole association

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:28.410
whereby an animal could
focus, could attend

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:32.030
to one aspect of something and
that aspect of the something,

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:35.080
of whatever it was, could
refer to the whole something,

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.010
even though it was a single aspect of it.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:40.560
What I'm describing here is
of course a rudimentary sign.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:42.230
It is semiosis in action.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:44.890
What comes about at this
moment is that the quality

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:48.200
or the aspect of the
broader set of stimuli

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.560
that is being focused on can come to refer

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:55.560
to that broader set, it can
come to mean that broader set.

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.063
It can come to be about that broader set.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:03.930
Here's an instance, here's
our European robin back again.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:05.862
This is a very intelligent animal,

00:17:05.862 --> 00:17:08.222
able to attend in all
kinds of complex ways

00:17:08.222 --> 00:17:09.470
to the world around it.

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.140
It has been fed during its childhood

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:14.510
by, say, regurgitated
food from its parents

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:15.900
but it has also attended to the fact

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.611
that its parents eat red berries.

00:17:17.611 --> 00:17:22.040
It has attended to this, but
by this recursive principle

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:23.041
that I've been talking about,

00:17:23.041 --> 00:17:25.901
it can also attend to
qualities of those berries,

00:17:25.901 --> 00:17:28.460
individual qualities, it
doesn't have to take on

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.150
every aspect of the berries to know

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.370
that those berries might
be something it might eat.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.190
It doesn't have to take on the
relationship of the berries

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.470
to the leaves or the
branches on which they grow.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.710
It doesn't have to take on many things,

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:42.940
but it might take on just
a single quality of them.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:44.847
Say, the redness of the berries, right?

00:17:44.847 --> 00:17:47.300
Once it does that, once it makes

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:49.400
a part for whole association
between the redness

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:51.540
and the berries as a whole, it has created

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:55.100
a simple sign of the berry,
a sign of what it eats,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.060
redness then can connote, can mean,

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.586
can refer to, what it eats.

00:17:59.586 --> 00:18:01.763
A sign and its object.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:05.900
Now, this is a very simple kind of sign.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.070
It is, in fact, what
Charles Sanders Peirce

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.900
famously called an icon.

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:12.200
It is the simplest kind
of sign, but it is one,

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:14.240
it is a simple sign, a foundation

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:16.780
from which a hierarchy of
more and more complex signs

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:17.870
will be built.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:19.830
So here's another instance of an icon.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.300
An icon then for Peirce, for me as well,

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:25.020
for Terrence Deacon, who is
referred to in this slide,

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:27.280
who was a neo-Peircian of the first order,

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:30.172
who has done all kinds of
analysis of Peircian semiosis,

00:18:30.172 --> 00:18:34.470
an icon is a sign that there's
a qualitative resemblance

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:35.774
to its object.

00:18:35.774 --> 00:18:38.750
In this case, we have a
painting of a woolly mammoth

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:42.070
on a cave wall, it's Pech
Merle in southern France.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:45.250
This refers to the woolly mammoth
itself, to the real thing.

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:47.070
It refers to the real thing by virtue

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.480
of only certain aspects
of that real thing,

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.700
the outline of its body, of its body shape

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:54.214
is what it uses, it doesn't require

00:18:54.214 --> 00:18:59.214
the the color of the wool
on the mammoth to be a part

00:18:59.231 --> 00:19:02.280
of the sign, it doesn't
require the color of the tusks.

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:03.900
It doesn't require any other facts

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.250
about the mammoth to be involved

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:08.070
in order to become an icon of the mammoth,

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.420
because it picks out one
qualitative resemblance

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:13.733
and associates that with the whole thing.

00:19:15.607 --> 00:19:19.160
The next more complex kind of sign,

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.300
and it rises in complexity
in part because animals

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:24.100
come along whose central nervous systems

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:27.370
enable them to construct
this greater complexity,

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:30.100
especially in their capacities
for learning and memory,

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:33.530
is what Peirce called the index.

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:35.930
And this is a sign that
strikes up a pointing

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.620
and indicating relation with its object,

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:40.810
a causal relation or just a relationship

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:43.190
of proximity in some cases.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.330
The most famous index of all
is smoke as an index of fire.

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:47.730
How does an index come about

00:19:48.627 --> 00:19:52.215
in the cognizance of an animal?

00:19:52.215 --> 00:19:56.010
Well it has to depend first of
all on some earlier learning

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.411
that smoke and fire were associated,

00:19:57.411 --> 00:19:59.980
so it is going to depend
already on some kind

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:02.560
of learning capacity, fairly
complicated learning capacity,

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:05.760
some kind of well-developed memory,

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:07.940
not just a transient working memory,

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:09.560
but some kind of episodic memory

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:12.330
that can remember episodes from
early in the animal's life.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:13.697
But once an animal has this,

00:20:13.697 --> 00:20:16.530
it can then strike up a relationship

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:19.651
between a present experience,
where there's smoke only,

00:20:19.651 --> 00:20:22.970
no fire that has been
perceived yet, and fire,

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.690
and an earlier experience
that was a complete experience

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:27.560
of smoke experienced with fire.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.140
It can put these two things together,

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:31.300
but notice that in putting
these two things together,

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:34.140
it has constructed a not
only an indexical sign,

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.130
but it has also depended on iconicity.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.030
It has depended on the way icons work,

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.710
because the present-day
incomplete experience

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:42.970
is made complete by making it an icon

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:45.120
of an earlier remembered
experience, right?

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:47.731
There's a qualitative
likeness of these two things,

00:20:47.731 --> 00:20:52.731
an index, smoke as
referring to meaning fire.

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:57.110
The most complex kind of symbol,

00:20:57.110 --> 00:21:00.600
the most complex kind of sign
for Peirce was the symbol.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.012
This is a kind of sign
that in the world today,

00:21:03.012 --> 00:21:06.210
to all our evidence, there are
no animals that make symbols

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:07.740
except for Homo sapiens.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:10.990
In an earlier world there may
have been some other hominins

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:14.270
that made symbols, Neanderthal,
Homo neanderthalensis,

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:15.697
the Neanderthals are the
best candidate for that.

00:21:15.697 --> 00:21:18.270
There was a very vexed
question as to whether

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.350
they were symbolic creatures or not.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:25.350
A symbol is a sign that relies
on a systematic relationship

00:21:26.798 --> 00:21:30.378
in a group of symbols in
order, that is somehow governed

00:21:30.378 --> 00:21:34.090
by rules, by conventions,
in order to to come

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:36.100
to its signifying function.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:39.030
It doesn't signify except
once it becomes a part

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:41.380
of some sort of organized system of signs.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:44.990
Now the most pervasive
and most foundational

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:48.750
and most important kind of symbolism

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:51.640
that modern humans all
practice is language,

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:54.250
and it is a hugely
developed and interesting

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.556
kind of symbolism, but
the words in a language,

00:21:56.556 --> 00:21:58.950
the words in a language
point to one another.

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:00.510
They point to one another
in all kinds of ways.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:02.840
Adverbs point to verbs,
verbs point to nouns,

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:05.310
pronouns point to nouns
or they might point

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:06.740
to whole groups of other words.

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.750
They can point in all
kinds of interesting ways,

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:12.137
all kinds of words are busy
pointing to other words.

00:22:12.137 --> 00:22:15.590
This, by the way, this pointing,
reminds us of the index

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:18.560
and we are going to find that
the symbol, just as the index,

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:20.940
was based on the icon for its structure.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:24.260
The symbol is based on the
index for its structure.

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.340
So first of all we have
indexical relationships,

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:28.770
pointing relationships,
among all the signs,

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:30.971
all the signs in a system of signs

00:22:30.971 --> 00:22:33.240
that will become symbols.

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:35.080
But by virtue of this
pointing relationship,

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:36.300
these indexical relationships,

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:38.000
the whole system, then, comes to be able

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.430
to point beyond itself,
for example to the world,

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:42.320
as language can point to the world.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.310
So we've got two kinds, two
orders, of indexicality here,

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.740
the indexicality that
governs the the relationships

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:50.610
within the system, the
indexicality that then enables

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:52.630
that governed system,
that rule-bound system,

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:54.953
to point beyond itself
to the world at large.

00:22:56.130 --> 00:23:00.540
Here we reach reach the apex
of the complexity of signs

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:06.480
and perhaps we will step back
and just have a quick review.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.580
We start from a foundation of information.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:14.030
Think of those messenger RNA
molecules and DNA molecules.

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:19.030
Semiosis grows out of a
broader realm of information.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.140
When in fact central
nervous systems come along

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:26.480
that are complex enough
to start performing

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.260
some of the functions that
i was just describing.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:31.083
Sorry, wrong direction.

00:23:31.083 --> 00:23:35.570
Culture, then, is a semiotic
enterprise through and through.

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:38.860
It is about signs and their aboutness.

00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:42.970
It uses, it takes as its stuff
signs and their aboutness.

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:45.880
And finally, in the most
complex kinds of signs,

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:48.840
the symbol, for example,
we see another possibility

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.800
within culture, that is to
say, complex cultural systems,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.060
systemic culture of one sort or another,

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:56.990
and we see also then,
to just review again,

00:23:56.990 --> 00:24:00.290
information is about
operative correspondence,

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.460
evolved operative correspondence.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.120
Semiosis is about aboutness

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:06.330
and that's where meaning comes into this.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.315
Culture is about learning
and transmission,

00:24:08.315 --> 00:24:09.970
and then we finally get to

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:13.253
the most complex kind of
cultures, systematic cultures.

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:17.980
So let's now turn to, am
I still going backwards,

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.900
I don't know, there,
there I'm going forward.

00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:24.850
Let's now turn to some
Paleolithic cultural systems

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:27.220
and think about how they are
semiotic and how they work.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:29.870
Here's an Acheulean hand-axe
and Acheulean biface,

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:32.530
this one from about 700,000 years ago.

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:34.800
It is a thing of beauty
to those of us who,

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:36.782
by the way, those of us
who tried to make one,

00:24:36.782 --> 00:24:38.552
boy is it hard,
(audience chuckles)

00:24:38.552 --> 00:24:41.080
and this is 700,000 years ago,

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:42.580
I don't even come close to Neanderthals,

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:45.571
but in any case, this technology,

00:24:45.571 --> 00:24:48.100
in order to make such a such a biface,

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:51.360
by the way, this technology
was already emerged

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:53.537
about 1 3/4 million years ago.

00:24:53.537 --> 00:24:56.840
Homo sapiens emerged between
300 and 200,000 years ago.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:58.580
This is a hugely ancient technology

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.770
practiced by many different
hominin species, right?

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:02.913
It goes way way back.

00:25:04.368 --> 00:25:06.360
In order to make one of these,

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:08.000
you take a cobble, you take a core,

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.180
and you take a hammer stone,

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:10.982
and you start chipping away at the cobble.

00:25:10.982 --> 00:25:13.106
When you chip away at the cobble,

00:25:13.106 --> 00:25:15.270
as anybody who has tried
this immediately learns,

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:16.920
when you, you cannot take a cobble

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:18.670
and say I know how an Acheulean hand-axe

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:20.050
is going to come out of this.

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:21.920
All you can do is chip away at the cobble,

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:25.202
and a flake is chipped off
and that flaking of the chip

00:25:25.202 --> 00:25:29.000
then forms an index pointing
you to the next gesture

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.660
and that forms an index
pointing you to the next gesture

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:33.290
and an index pointing
you to the next gesture.

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:37.070
At every moment, you are
concatenating signs, indexes,

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:39.750
from one gesture to
the next that help you,

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:41.950
finally, if you you're pretty good at it,

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:43.700
get to something as
beautifully symmetrical

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:46.470
as as this hand-axe is.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:50.120
This is, then, a pretty
complex array of indexes

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:52.720
that enables this cultural
system to be passed along

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:54.610
and to be taught and transmitted

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:55.940
from generation to generation,

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.425
but notice that it's just a
kind of a willy-nilly sequence

00:25:58.425 --> 00:26:03.410
of indexes, it is not
a sequence of indexes

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:06.660
that is somehow prearranged,
that is somehow hierarchical,

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:08.080
that is organized in more

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:11.830
than a very very rudimentary manner.

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:14.560
If we look at a later stone technology,

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:18.260
Levallois point making, this
now is the stone technology

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:22.310
that came into existence,
the debates raged,

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:24.840
somewhere between 300 and
400,000 years ago maybe,

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:27.060
somewhere on that time scale at any rate.

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:29.180
It has been studied to a fare-the-well

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:32.100
within association with Neanderthals.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:34.210
It's also been studied a
lot recently in association

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:35.972
with our forebears in Africa,

00:26:35.972 --> 00:26:38.368
Neanderthals of course in Europe,

00:26:38.368 --> 00:26:40.000
and here's how it works.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.640
You take the cobble now and from 1 to 2,

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.110
what you're doing is
you're taking a cobble

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:46.430
and you're chipping off, flicking flakes

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:47.610
in a rather systematic way

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:49.530
around the edge of the cobble, right?

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:52.410
Once you do that, you're
in, you're prepared now,

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.300
but because you've
formed sharp edges there,

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:57.350
you are prepared to begin
to form a convex surface

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.540
on the top of the cobble by, in number 3,

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:03.120
chipping off, flaking off bits
on the top of the cobble now

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:05.705
and in number 4 that process is completed.

00:27:05.705 --> 00:27:09.530
And if you do this right,
in number 5 you may need

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:13.000
one or more other strokes, kinds
of kinds of hammer strokes.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:14.910
You may need to lop off one end of it,

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:16.740
depending upon the shape
of the cobble and so on,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.730
but if you've done this
right, one strike here,

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:21.550
represented by the arrow in number 5,

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:24.030
will break off a finished point,

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:25.490
or at least a point that only needs

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.350
a slight retouching, right?

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:30.330
Now think about this as a
system, a system of indexes.

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.620
First of all, it is systematic in a way

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:34.230
that the Acheulean biface indexes

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:35.290
were not at all systematic.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:37.520
This is not a willy-nilly
array of indexes,

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:39.990
one following after another
in an unpredictable fashion.

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:43.260
It is a set of hierarchized
different kinds of indexes

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:46.861
in regard to the core
that you started with

00:27:46.861 --> 00:27:49.100
that have to be arranged in just this way

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:51.609
or you will not get to the final product.

00:27:51.609 --> 00:27:53.840
The point will not will not come off,

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:55.700
it as those of us who have tried

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:58.995
to do this Levallois technology
learned very quickly.

00:27:58.995 --> 00:28:01.215
It is very very hard to get this right,

00:28:01.215 --> 00:28:06.215
but it's quite a sophisticated technology.

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:12.360
The fact that all of
these indexes are arranged

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:17.360
in this hierarchized set of
different kinds of indexes

00:28:17.735 --> 00:28:19.470
that have to be ordered just so

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:22.390
in order to get the final
product is why I think of this

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:24.040
not just as an indexical system,

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:26.070
but as a hyper-indexical system.

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:28.043
Something has happened here
in the ordering of indexes

00:28:28.043 --> 00:28:30.290
that is decisively
different from what happened

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:32.093
in the Acheulean hand-axe.

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:37.010
Okay what we have now, by
this time is, is hominins,

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.500
several species of hominins,
we keep discovering

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.360
more and more probably,
but, and just one last week

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:44.416
with Homo luzonensis
that you may have noticed

00:28:44.416 --> 00:28:48.430
in the papers, but at this
point in the evolution

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:53.040
of hominins, we've got a
lot of complex semiosis

00:28:54.504 --> 00:28:56.410
that is going on that is being transmitted

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.410
from generation to
generation in the culture.

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:02.030
We've got cultural systems
of some complexity.

00:29:02.030 --> 00:29:04.440
What is the impact of these
cultural systems, then,

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:06.960
on the niche construction,
on our second algorithm,

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.960
on that cultural niche construction

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:10.410
we were talking about before?

00:29:11.439 --> 00:29:13.530
Well here's a diagram
that adds now something

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:16.460
down at the bottom that
tries to show this.

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:17.924
What happens with these cultural systems

00:29:17.924 --> 00:29:19.930
is that even though they are generated

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:22.240
by the overall cycle, the feedback cycle,

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:24.640
of niche construction in cultural animals,

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:26.680
even though they are
generated from that cycle,

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:29.400
they come to have a wholistic
quality, first of all,

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:30.630
because they have to be passed on,

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.080
think of the Levallois technology,

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:34.870
it must be passed on as a
whole or it doesn't work.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.565
Because of this wholistic transmission,

00:29:37.565 --> 00:29:41.730
their integrated nature as a
system comes to be more marked

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:43.276
in the cultures that practice them.

00:29:43.276 --> 00:29:45.396
They have an autonomy
from the other aspects

00:29:45.396 --> 00:29:48.685
of the cultural practices of the culture.

00:29:48.685 --> 00:29:51.570
They have a robustness of
systematic integration,

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:52.530
perhaps, as well.

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:55.960
All of these things form what
I call cultural epicycles.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:59.900
They form cultural forces,
technologies in this case,

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:03.120
cultural forces that that
that kind of come to stand

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:06.320
outside of the overall feedback
loop of niche construction.

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:09.580
Because they are so autonomous
in their integrated wholism,

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:11.640
they come to stand outside
of that feedback loop

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:14.510
and they come then to be able
to feed forward into the loop

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:16.200
and bias it and redirect it

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:18.593
and change its direction, finally, right?

00:30:19.626 --> 00:30:21.800
A simple example of this
would be the control of fire.

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:23.390
One need only think for a minute to think

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:26.868
that's a technology, it's a
pretty complex technology,

00:30:26.868 --> 00:30:31.110
probably mastered between
700,000 and a million years ago

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:32.760
by hominins, though the dating

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:34.950
is again another vexed problem,

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.260
but nonetheless think
of the control of fire

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:38.730
and think of what that would do

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:42.120
once there was a
systematic control of fire,

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:45.864
think what it would do
in terms of redirecting

00:30:45.864 --> 00:30:47.823
the niche construction, right?

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:50.986
All right, cultural epicycles,

00:30:50.986 --> 00:30:55.426
redirecting cultural niche construction.

00:30:55.426 --> 00:30:59.543
Let's look at such an epicycle,
the bead-making epicycle.

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:04.060
Bead making is hugely
dispersed in hominin cultures,

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:06.050
probably only in Homo sapiens culture,

00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:08.050
but maybe also in Neanderthal cultures,

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:09.730
in any case in sapient culture,

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:11.780
in our own ancestors' cultures,

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:15.037
and related sapient groups,
it goes back at least

00:31:15.037 --> 00:31:18.130
100,000 years, probably
more like 130,000 years,

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.200
what archeologists mean by bead making

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:22.370
is to take any small object, a seashell,

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:26.085
a fragment of an ostrich egg
shell in another instance,

00:31:26.085 --> 00:31:28.890
a tooth of an animal that has been hunted,

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:30.840
a piece of mineral, a piece of amber,

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:33.500
take any small object and
drill it through or pierce it

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:36.500
so as to be hung, so as
to be hung on a strap,

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:38.210
an anklet, a bracelet, a necklace,

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:41.253
so as to be hung on hair, so
as to be hung on clothing.

00:31:43.082 --> 00:31:46.540
Bead making, why, the
mystery about bead making is

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:50.250
why does it pop up
everywhere Homo sapiens goes?

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:52.590
In in vastly different
geographical arenas,

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:55.310
vastly different niches,
bead making pops up.

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:57.168
What is making this epicycle,

00:31:57.168 --> 00:32:00.480
what is the force that makes
this epicycle take shape?

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:03.010
For me it has to do
precisely with the complexity

00:32:03.010 --> 00:32:04.700
of semiotic cognition.

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:07.978
What you've got now is early humans

00:32:07.978 --> 00:32:10.690
who are who are working with indexes

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:11.770
in pretty complicated ways.

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:16.540
We see that already in the
Levallois point making,

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:19.596
but you've also, that complicated semiosis

00:32:19.596 --> 00:32:23.470
must also be reflected in
the growing complications

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:26.127
of the social structures
of these groups themselves.

00:32:26.127 --> 00:32:30.080
What is probably coming
on board are new registers

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:33.537
of kinship relations, new
registers of social status

00:32:33.537 --> 00:32:35.273
and social power, right?

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:37.640
But as these things come on board,

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:40.180
as the complexity of the society grows,

00:32:40.180 --> 00:32:41.579
what is also manifesting itself

00:32:41.579 --> 00:32:45.810
are invisible realms of
culture, so to speak.

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:47.800
These are not realms that
are necessarily smacking you

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:51.249
in the face, you cannot
tell that somebody is a kin,

00:32:51.249 --> 00:32:55.290
and especially a distant kin,
simply by looking at them,

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:56.830
simply by encountering them.

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:59.680
This is now something that
needs, that has become invisible

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:01.360
in the practice of culture itself

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:03.660
and needs then to be made visible.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:07.870
What could be more more easily understood

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:09.810
than that these cultures
would, all of them,

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.553
one after another, use their
sophisticated technologies

00:33:12.553 --> 00:33:17.060
so as to construct markers
by which social difference

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:18.251
could be registered.

00:33:18.251 --> 00:33:20.211
Now these were no doubt,
these beads were no doubt

00:33:20.211 --> 00:33:21.970
not the only social markers.

00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:26.970
They scarification of the
flesh, body painting, tattooing,

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:28.650
there may have been many, clothing,

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:30.100
the kinds of clothing that was worn,

00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:32.160
it's just that meat making
is the one that was made

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:35.470
with materials that have
endured down to our time

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:37.570
and can be studied by archeologists.

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:40.368
But it shows us, it seems
to me, the way in which

00:33:40.368 --> 00:33:43.980
a cultural epicycle can become necessary

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:46.923
in the growing complexity of
the cultural niche construction

00:33:46.923 --> 00:33:48.453
of a group of humans.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:51.130
It shows us something else, too.

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:53.760
It shows us, because if
we take a group, say,

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.848
this is a group that has been studied

00:33:55.848 --> 00:33:58.610
or at least an instance
that has been studied,

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:01.856
if we take humans 100,000
years ago in Blombos Cave

00:34:01.856 --> 00:34:03.948
on the southern edge of South Africa,

00:34:03.948 --> 00:34:07.090
they've been harvesting
shellfish for their food

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:09.350
for a long long time now, for
tens of thousands of years

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:11.170
maybe they've been harvesting shellfish.

00:34:11.170 --> 00:34:13.130
When they come to be a bead-making culture

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:15.542
using the seashells as their beads,

00:34:15.542 --> 00:34:19.850
suddenly a new affordance
from the, a new resource

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.490
in the niche has been
invented, so to speak,

00:34:22.490 --> 00:34:24.060
by their cultural practice, right?

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:26.630
Seashells are no longer trash
to be thrown on the mitten.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.400
Seashells now have the capacity

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:30.763
to become signs of social complexity.

00:34:31.740 --> 00:34:36.740
So what happens in this
epicyclic niche construction

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:39.900
is not only the the redirecting

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:42.540
of the cycle of niche construction

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:44.760
okay in all kinds of possible directions,

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:46.600
but in the course of that redirecting,

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:48.460
the uncovering of new resources,

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:51.290
perhaps the meeting up
against of new constraints

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:53.200
in the environment, all
kinds of things can happen

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:55.140
in terms of the
affordances and constraints

00:34:55.140 --> 00:34:56.283
of the environment.

00:34:57.270 --> 00:35:00.227
Here's another kind of, this
is a whole class of epicycle,

00:35:00.227 --> 00:35:05.227
of cultural epicycles, as far
as I see, and that is ritual.

00:35:05.545 --> 00:35:07.040
So what is a ritual?

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:08.470
Well, first of all, it is behavior

00:35:08.470 --> 00:35:11.120
that has been systematized,
it's repeatable behavior.

00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:13.763
It's distinct by its
systematic organization

00:35:13.763 --> 00:35:18.290
from everyday activities that
don't somehow count as ritual.

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:20.100
In all of these features,
it begins to look

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:23.365
like the wholistic systems
of cultural epicycles,

00:35:23.365 --> 00:35:26.180
and, like epicycles, it might reshape

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:28.325
the affordances and constraints,

00:35:28.325 --> 00:35:33.325
even by using the materials as a part

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:36.540
of its distinct cultural system.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:40.630
It might it might reshape the
affordances and constraints

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:43.690
of the environment in the
way that I was just saying.

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:45.480
The autonomy and robustness of the system

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:48.780
might be very much marked by
the systematized repeatable

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:50.770
nature of it, but there's
one new ingredient

00:35:50.770 --> 00:35:51.760
that we haven't introduced yet

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:54.200
that is absolutely basic to ritual,

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:57.374
and that is that there's
a double systematization

00:35:57.374 --> 00:35:58.830
at stake here.

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:01.640
On the one hand, there are
the elements of the ritual

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:04.870
that are systematized in
relationship to one another,

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:07.210
but on the other hand the
relationship between the ritual

00:36:07.210 --> 00:36:10.100
and the broader culture
is systematized, as well.

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:12.960
You do not have a funerary
ritual unless somebody has died.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:14.730
You don't have a puberty
ritual unless somebody

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:16.086
is passing through puberty.

00:36:16.086 --> 00:36:19.230
So that double systematization
is, it seems to me,

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:21.971
crucial in a definition of ritual

00:36:21.971 --> 00:36:24.540
and in trying to understand
the origins of ritual.

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:26.010
And it suggests that those origins

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.340
are very very ancient origins, in fact.

00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:31.470
It suggests to me that
what we need in a culture

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:34.680
for it to practice ritual
is hyper-indexicality.

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:36.310
It is not yet symbolism.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:39.068
You don't have to have language
in order to practice ritual,

00:36:39.068 --> 00:36:42.850
and I think we can see
what you do have to have

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:46.344
is a performed system of indexes.

00:36:46.344 --> 00:36:48.900
We've seen perform systems
of indexes, after all,

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:50.300
in Levallois technology.

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:53.810
What you have to have also
is the indexical relation

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:55.780
of that system to the broader culture,

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:58.220
the hyper-indexical doubleness
I was talking about.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:00.180
All of this brings you to
the verge of symbolism,

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.740
but it doesn't necessarily
require symbolism.

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:04.740
You don't need language for ritual.

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:06.532
This is an idea that is rather foreign

00:37:06.532 --> 00:37:09.240
to many anthropologists when
they think about ritual,

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:12.010
but it seems to me that we can look back

00:37:12.010 --> 00:37:16.270
deep in our deep history and see creatures

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:19.495
who were not entirely
like us in many many ways

00:37:19.495 --> 00:37:21.500
and yet had already, had begun

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:24.400
to form rudimentary ritual
structures in their societies,

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:26.050
and one of the ways they were not like us

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:28.640
was they did not yet have modern language.

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:31.940
They had proto languages
of indexical sorts,

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.470
complex no doubt in their
communicative capacities,

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:37.544
but nothing like the full
grammar syntax and lexicon

00:37:37.544 --> 00:37:40.270
that makes language the symbolic

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:42.850
medium par excellence that it is.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:46.835
Here's an instance of that
rather lurid picture of.

00:37:46.835 --> 00:37:51.050
This is a representation of a site,

00:37:51.050 --> 00:37:53.090
Boxgrove in southern England,

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:55.900
that has been excavated and
excavated and excavated.

00:37:55.900 --> 00:37:57.740
It was a stunning site when it was found.

00:37:57.740 --> 00:38:00.960
What was found on this site was evidence

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:05.310
of hominin hunting, apparently,
and also butchering,

00:38:05.310 --> 00:38:07.528
then, of a rhinoceros, of several horses,

00:38:07.528 --> 00:38:10.610
a whole system, a cultural system.

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:13.300
There was a waterhole, you
can see that in the picture.

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:14.810
I love this kind of picture, by the way,

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:17.214
this kind of peaceable
kingdom, right, with a twist.

00:38:17.214 --> 00:38:19.330
All these animals kind
of wandering around,

00:38:19.330 --> 00:38:21.660
kind of, you know, happily
sort of looking at them.

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:24.540
But, nonetheless, whatever
is not necessarily

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:28.293
what Boxgrove would have
looked like 500,000 years ago.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:31.560
Some of these things are what
this would have looked like

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.690
at the moment of
butchering this rhinoceros.

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.510
Number one, we know that this
person making stone tools,

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:38.130
stone tools were made on the site

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:39.550
in order to butcher the rhinoceros.

00:38:39.550 --> 00:38:40.890
We know where the stones came from.

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:43.580
They came from the limestone
cliffs at the back.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:47.140
We even have places
where cobbles were picked

00:38:47.140 --> 00:38:49.450
off the limestone cliffs
and tested right there

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:51.070
and deemed not worthy of being carried

00:38:51.070 --> 00:38:52.360
over to the to the rhinoceros.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:54.750
That too has been analyzed
by these archeologists.

00:38:54.750 --> 00:38:59.060
This site has just revealed a
treasure trove of information.

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:01.240
We can imagine, then, the complexity of

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:03.232
and the fraught complexity of this scene,

00:39:03.232 --> 00:39:05.830
which is pictured here, as well.

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:09.370
You've got some of the humans
that need to be warding off

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:10.940
the hyenas and the scavengers

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.710
that are going to be surrounding them.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.690
You've got others that are
making the stone tools.

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:17.400
Some of the food would be
consumed immediately on the scene.

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:19.070
Some of the suit food
would be lugged away,

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:20.518
probably to be cooked.

00:39:20.518 --> 00:39:22.678
A lot of complexity to this, right?

00:39:22.678 --> 00:39:25.350
It is not necessarily a ritual scene,

00:39:25.350 --> 00:39:27.840
and yet we are in a
hyper-indexical situation

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:29.970
that could even easily
give rise to ritual.

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:31.820
And we are 500,000 years ago.

00:39:31.820 --> 00:39:35.727
200,000 years and more
before Homo sapiens emerged,

00:39:35.727 --> 00:39:39.510
150,000 thousand years or so
before Neanderthals emerged.

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:42.088
This would have been Homo
heidelbergensis in all likelihood,

00:39:42.088 --> 00:39:44.300
usually taken to be the common ancestor

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:47.193
of both Homo sapiens and Neanderthals.

00:39:48.728 --> 00:39:51.593
Okay, another review.

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:55.410
Again, we look at the pyramid.

00:39:56.305 --> 00:39:58.140
The fundamental information

00:39:58.140 --> 00:39:59.540
with its correspondence at the bottom.

00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:01.670
The semiosis with its aboutness meaning.

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:04.241
The culture with its learning
and teaching transmission.

00:40:04.241 --> 00:40:05.920
Complex cultural systems,

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:08.930
epicyclic niche construction
is involved there,

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:11.370
but notice where I've
arrayed the kinds of signs

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:12.760
that we talked about at first.

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:16.900
Culture can subsist perfectly
well with icons and indexes.

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:19.440
I don't think cultural animals
can subsist with icons alone,

00:40:19.440 --> 00:40:22.810
but with icons and indexes,
culture can emerge.

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:25.730
For complex systemic
culture, you need at least

00:40:25.730 --> 00:40:27.900
the hyper index, as I'm calling it,

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:29.410
or perhaps full-fledged symbolism.

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:32.270
Of course, my view is you do
not need full-fledged symbolism

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:34.490
for this cultural complex idiom to emerge.

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:37.440
Hyper-indexicality is
enough, and indeed my view

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.838
is that hyper-indexicality
lasted a long long time

00:40:39.838 --> 00:40:41.510
in Homo sapiens' history,

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:43.750
and it was only after
the 100,000 year mark

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:45.840
that something, that that modern language

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:48.240
fully coalesced, it coalesced,

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:51.363
a rather recent
coalescing, in other words.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:56.450
And now, again, to think again, once more,

00:40:56.450 --> 00:41:00.400
about the way in which epicycles
bias niche construction.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:02.020
Here's one more chart.

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:04.280
You've got a complex
ordered cultural archive,

00:41:04.280 --> 00:41:06.180
think of the Levallois
technology that I've shown you

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:07.900
up in the in the right-hand corner.

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.140
This brings about hierarchy systems,

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:12.430
wholistic transmission,
robustness, autonomy,

00:41:12.430 --> 00:41:14.070
all these things I've
talked about already.

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:16.420
Cultural epicycles with
their feed forward bias

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:18.970
that changes the course
of niche construction.

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:22.532
New affordances and constraints
that then can then be

00:41:22.532 --> 00:41:26.420
identified and exploited
in in niche construction.

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:28.790
Feeding back into a niche that is now

00:41:28.790 --> 00:41:32.116
going to be constructed in a
new and different way, right?

00:41:32.116 --> 00:41:34.250
All of this process, it seems to me,

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:37.320
is a process that, in in our deep history,

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:40.400
began easily by 100,000 years ago,

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:41.490
probably longer than that,

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:42.820
probably a good deal longer than that,

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:45.850
began to open further
domains, so to speak,

00:41:45.850 --> 00:41:48.630
of cultural activity for hominins,

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:51.200
not only Homo sapiens but but a wide group

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.618
of different kinds of hominins, probably.

00:41:53.618 --> 00:41:55.300
And I want to finish by talking then

00:41:55.300 --> 00:41:57.030
about some of those new domains.

00:41:57.030 --> 00:41:58.782
Some of these reach back beyond,

00:41:58.782 --> 00:42:01.170
farther beyond Homo sapiens.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.660
Some of them are sapient, in particular,

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:05.420
but all of them show the encroaching

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:09.369
of this powerful epicyclic
niche construction

00:42:09.369 --> 00:42:13.876
that can simply expand out,
expand the possibilities

00:42:13.876 --> 00:42:16.513
of the building of the niche.

00:42:17.698 --> 00:42:20.860
So here's the first of them,
hyperdeveloped technology.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:22.020
Well, this might seem obvious, right,

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:23.912
we've talked a lot about technology today.

00:42:23.912 --> 00:42:28.382
The notion of hyperdeveloped
technology as a new domain

00:42:28.382 --> 00:42:30.500
of niche construction is one

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:32.440
that comes on board pretty early.

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:33.330
Here's an example.

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:36.320
These are a couple of
the Schoningen spears,

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:40.660
wooden javelins that were
unearthed in the 1990s in Germany.

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:43.880
Well over 300,000 years
old by the most recent

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:47.200
and authoritative dating.

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:50.260
This is long long before
Homo sapiens made it

00:42:50.260 --> 00:42:53.700
in any persistent way in Europe.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.610
It is right at the beginning

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:58.320
of the of the Neanderthal
history in Europe.

00:42:58.320 --> 00:42:59.500
These are very very ancient.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:00.800
They don't, they may not look to you

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.260
like hyperdeveloped technology,

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:04.160
however these javelins, first of all,

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:06.540
they are aerodynamically
wonderful, number one.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:09.320
Number two, they were carved
out of the hardest wood

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:11.428
at the bottom of a spruce trunk.

00:43:11.428 --> 00:43:13.840
This is all being done
with stone tools, right,

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:17.070
this is an extraordinarily
hyperdeveloped technology

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:18.670
to get something like this.

00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:21.050
The fact that these wooden
spears were, of course,

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:24.802
wood is not very durable in
terms of archeological spans

00:43:24.802 --> 00:43:26.390
of like 300,000 years.

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:29.040
The fact that they were
preserved is a small miracle

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:30.043
in and of itself.

00:43:31.511 --> 00:43:34.493
Another hyperdeveloped
technology, the bow and arrow.

00:43:35.382 --> 00:43:38.160
The bow and arrow is now
a very very complicated

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.370
composite technology.

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:42.190
The arrow itself needs
needs feathers at the end.

00:43:42.190 --> 00:43:45.915
It needs it needs a point at
that is hafted onto the shaft

00:43:45.915 --> 00:43:48.490
at the forward end.

00:43:48.490 --> 00:43:50.900
The bow is another complex composite tool.

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:53.920
The recent notions of
bow and arrow technology

00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:55.510
that archeologists have
been putting forward

00:43:55.510 --> 00:43:58.240
suggested in Africa, this may go back,

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:00.400
by some estimates, 500,000 years.

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:02.490
That's a pretty large estimate.

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:05.813
Most people sort of settle
well over 100,000 years ago,

00:44:05.813 --> 00:44:08.370
somewhere between 100
and 500,000 years ago.

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:10.510
We don't have specimens
of bows and arrows.

00:44:10.510 --> 00:44:13.180
All we have are points
that seem to have been used

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:15.370
to make arrows in some cases, right?

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:17.070
The stone points are
the only parts of these

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:19.610
that have survived, but the technology

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:21.750
for making these points
goes back something like

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:23.453
300,000 years or more.

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:28.940
Here's the astonishing
Gabammung rock shelter

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:31.540
in Amhem Land in the the
Northern Territory of Australia

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:33.930
probably between 28 and 48,000 years ago.

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:34.920
I'm not showing it to you

00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:37.960
for these amazing amazing
paintings all over the ceiling

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:42.960
of this naturally eroded away
kind of cathedral effect here,

00:44:45.050 --> 00:44:47.180
I'm showing it to you because
we need to pay attention

00:44:47.180 --> 00:44:49.900
to Australia when we talk about
hyperdeveloped technologies.

00:44:49.900 --> 00:44:52.497
The latest evidence, which
just was published last year,

00:44:52.497 --> 00:44:56.580
has pretty conclusively settled the date

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:59.740
by which Homo sapiens
made it to Australia.

00:44:59.740 --> 00:45:01.591
60 to 65,000 years ago.

00:45:01.591 --> 00:45:04.060
This is hugely ancient, first of all,

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:06.640
and second of all, they
have made it to Australia

00:45:06.640 --> 00:45:08.960
at a time when, while
there was never a time

00:45:08.960 --> 00:45:11.705
when Australia was connected
to the Indonesian islands

00:45:11.705 --> 00:45:14.123
by a land bridge, right.

00:45:14.123 --> 00:45:16.413
At the periods of greatest glaciation,

00:45:16.413 --> 00:45:18.660
when the sea levels were lowest,

00:45:18.660 --> 00:45:20.925
there were still something
like 70 kilometers of open sea

00:45:20.925 --> 00:45:22.470
to get across.

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:24.800
These people were
seafarers, in some fashion,

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:26.900
and it's utterly astonishing
that they could be.

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:31.010
We have no record, because
we'd have no preserved boats

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:34.270
or anything that looks
like boat technology

00:45:34.270 --> 00:45:36.040
from anywhere in the Paleolithic era.

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:38.380
It would be wonderful to
find it, they were using it,

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:40.580
and it might be that Homo luzonensis,

00:45:40.580 --> 00:45:43.220
announced just last week,
was also a seafaring creature

00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:45.477
from an entirely different
lineage than Homo sapiens,

00:45:45.477 --> 00:45:48.120
that, too, there was no
land bridge to Luzon,

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:50.300
the Philippine island
where where those fossils

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:51.913
were discovered, ever, either.

00:45:52.991 --> 00:45:54.775
All right, further new domains.

00:45:54.775 --> 00:45:56.810
The release from proximity.

00:45:56.810 --> 00:45:58.550
This is a great phrase that was introduced

00:45:58.550 --> 00:46:00.460
by an archeologist and
a number of years ago.

00:46:00.460 --> 00:46:03.807
What it means is the establishment
of the offline imaginary

00:46:03.807 --> 00:46:06.530
or the offline imagination in some way.

00:46:06.530 --> 00:46:10.171
Think of the capacity of baboons
to interact in complex ways

00:46:10.171 --> 00:46:11.550
socially with one another.

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:15.200
It's a very complex sociality
but it's all face to face.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:18.140
They interact according to
face-to-face transactions.

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:20.640
The notion of, the question of when it is

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:23.290
in our hominin evolution,
when it is that hominins

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:26.919
moved beyond that notion into an imaginary

00:46:26.919 --> 00:46:29.060
that could could posit interactions

00:46:29.060 --> 00:46:30.160
that might come tomorrow,

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.420
could remember interactions
that came yesterday

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:34.714
and work on the basis of those,

00:46:34.714 --> 00:46:39.410
could begin to extend the
imagination far beyond the senses.

00:46:39.410 --> 00:46:42.120
That's what this release
from proximity is about.

00:46:42.120 --> 00:46:44.400
We see it in cave paintings,

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:46.470
the very notion of painting
this fabulous panel

00:46:46.470 --> 00:46:48.040
from Grotte Chauvet, the very notion

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:53.040
of painting animals on on a
wall with these iconic patterns

00:46:53.112 --> 00:46:54.480
of the sort that we talked about

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:56.482
with the woolly mammoth at the beginning,

00:46:56.482 --> 00:46:59.330
when the animals are not
there, the animals are outside,

00:46:59.330 --> 00:47:01.270
is a really a release from proximity

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:02.920
of an extraordinary sort in itself.

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:04.670
But much earlier than Grotte Chauvet,

00:47:04.670 --> 00:47:07.120
we see something like this
incised piece of ochre

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:09.760
from Blombos Cave in South Africa.

00:47:09.760 --> 00:47:12.703
More than 70,000 years ago
was when it was incised.

00:47:12.703 --> 00:47:15.730
Incised for what purpose, we don't know,

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:18.020
but incised with some
sort of intent, clearly,

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:21.570
and this was not a haphazard
incision that was done.

00:47:21.570 --> 00:47:23.930
This, too, has been studied,
very microscopically,

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:25.940
in fact, that there are a
number of pieces like this.

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:27.856
They've been studied
microscopically to understand

00:47:27.856 --> 00:47:29.260
how they came about.

00:47:29.260 --> 00:47:31.076
It didn't come about from
somebody chopping meat

00:47:31.076 --> 00:47:33.410
on top of on top of a piece of ochre.

00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:35.500
It came about because for
some reason this ochre

00:47:35.500 --> 00:47:38.961
incised in this way
could have some meaning

00:47:38.961 --> 00:47:41.823
beyond its presence as a piece of ochre.

00:47:41.823 --> 00:47:43.540
I've already talked about beads.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:45.930
Here are some of the shell
beads from Blombos Cave,

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:47.837
also more than 70,000 years ago.

00:47:47.837 --> 00:47:51.520
This, too, is the notion
of those invisible aspects

00:47:51.520 --> 00:47:53.980
of sociality, is another case in point

00:47:53.980 --> 00:47:55.563
of the release from proximity.

00:47:57.760 --> 00:47:59.240
One of the most famous instances of this,

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:02.530
over 40,000 years old,
from southwestern Germany,

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:03.620
the so-called Lowenmensch,

00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:06.700
the lion man of Hohlenstein-Stadel.

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:10.370
This is an extraordinary
carved ivory, mammoth ivory,

00:48:10.370 --> 00:48:14.360
about this tall, of a creature

00:48:14.360 --> 00:48:16.048
that could never have been seen.

00:48:16.048 --> 00:48:19.950
For which one had to have a
dramatic release from proximity

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:22.110
in order to come up with the
notion in the first place.

00:48:22.110 --> 00:48:26.420
Half human or humanoid, bipedal, upright,

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:28.230
but with a lion head at the top of it,

00:48:28.230 --> 00:48:30.100
or a feline head at the top of it.

00:48:30.100 --> 00:48:33.585
So, the release from proximity.

00:48:33.585 --> 00:48:36.360
The release from proximity
is working hand-in-hand

00:48:36.360 --> 00:48:37.460
with what I've already talked about,

00:48:37.460 --> 00:48:39.690
the doubled indexicality of ritual.

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:42.210
This complex doubled
indexicality is bringing you,

00:48:42.210 --> 00:48:44.157
as I said before, close to the notion

00:48:44.157 --> 00:48:46.480
of a full fledged symbolism.

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:48.560
And here, for my illustration,
well I can't resist

00:48:48.560 --> 00:48:50.460
giving this crazy painting from 1883,

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.000
Viktor Vasnetsov, Russian
landscape painter,

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:55.700
and painted Russian scenes
and every once in a while,

00:48:55.700 --> 00:48:58.989
he indulged himself and
painted a stone-age scene.

00:48:58.989 --> 00:49:01.310
It's an extraordinary thing,

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:05.393
almost like that peaceable
kingdom we saw a few slides ago.

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:08.609
The doubled indexicality of ritual,

00:49:08.609 --> 00:49:12.270
hyper hyper-indexicality. (chuckles)

00:49:12.270 --> 00:49:14.070
What do I mean by hyper
hyper-indexicality?

00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:14.950
Well you heard at the beginning

00:49:14.950 --> 00:49:16.420
that I'm a musicologist by trade

00:49:16.420 --> 00:49:18.879
and so music had to enter
in here at some point.

00:49:18.879 --> 00:49:20.879
- [Audience Member] Yes.

00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:24.140
- This is a single pipe viewed

00:49:24.140 --> 00:49:26.867
from several different vantages.

00:49:26.867 --> 00:49:31.867
Again, constructed from
a griffon vulture radius,

00:49:32.120 --> 00:49:33.330
a wing bone

00:49:33.330 --> 00:49:34.280
of a griffon vulture.
- Ulna.

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:35.900
- You say ulna?
- It's an ulna.

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:38.810
- It's an ulna, goodness, thank you, Rick.

00:49:38.810 --> 00:49:41.468
(audience laughing)

00:49:41.468 --> 00:49:44.966
Any bird information, he's
the man, he is the man.

00:49:44.966 --> 00:49:47.483
Thank you, Rick, it is an ulna.

00:49:48.487 --> 00:49:52.920
In any case, this fantastic
pipe over 40,000 years old,

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:55.863
the reason that I call music
hyper hyper-indexicality

00:49:55.863 --> 00:49:58.863
is because, though music
in the modern world,

00:49:58.863 --> 00:50:03.060
all human music, is absolutely
imbricated and tied in with

00:50:03.060 --> 00:50:06.533
and overlaid on and
interweaved with symbolism,

00:50:06.533 --> 00:50:10.509
it cannot not be because we
are absolutely inveterate

00:50:10.509 --> 00:50:13.864
habitual symbolic creatures
today in the world.

00:50:13.864 --> 00:50:17.830
So music is always
involved with symbolic acts

00:50:17.830 --> 00:50:21.264
in modern humanity, but the music itself,

00:50:21.264 --> 00:50:23.210
in the nature of its construction,

00:50:23.210 --> 00:50:24.730
musical systems across the world,

00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:26.320
in the nature of their construction

00:50:26.320 --> 00:50:30.600
and in the nature of the the
cognitive reception of them

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:33.044
and processing of them can be thought of

00:50:33.044 --> 00:50:36.000
not in fact as symbolic at all,

00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:39.504
but as the most hyperdeveloped
kind of ordered indexes

00:50:39.504 --> 00:50:41.890
that humans have invented,

00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:44.169
hence the hyper hyper-indexicality.

00:50:44.169 --> 00:50:48.630
Music is is indexical on many
different levels at once,

00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:50.930
even what music that we would think of

00:50:50.930 --> 00:50:54.570
as simple music is this,
indexical on many different levels

00:50:54.570 --> 00:50:57.460
at once, different temporal
levels, different scales

00:50:57.460 --> 00:51:00.170
at which things are
pointing to one another

00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:01.330
and indicating one another,

00:51:01.330 --> 00:51:03.350
indicating things that
might or might not come

00:51:03.350 --> 00:51:05.650
with the next gesture in the music,

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:07.303
hyper hyper-indexicality.

00:51:09.690 --> 00:51:11.270
Full-fledged symbolism, of course,

00:51:11.270 --> 00:51:14.330
the best example of this is language.

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:17.273
Here's a map of language
families across the world.

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:24.240
And finally, emerging cultural difference,

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:27.100
slash, extended niche construction.

00:51:27.100 --> 00:51:29.240
Why have I linked these
two things together?

00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:31.574
Well perhaps it is clear
from what I've said already,

00:51:31.574 --> 00:51:34.920
but the human capacity to
construct cultural difference

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:38.650
had to have emerged in relation

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:42.310
to systematic epicyclic
niche construction.

00:51:42.310 --> 00:51:45.410
The systematized epicyclic epicycles

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:47.580
of the niche construction is pointing up

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:49.540
new resources, new possibilities.

00:51:49.540 --> 00:51:54.500
It is then tapping into the
the behavioral flexibility

00:51:54.500 --> 00:51:58.170
of these cultural
creatures, who then devise,

00:51:58.170 --> 00:51:59.700
according to these new resources,

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:01.810
new strategies for niche construction.

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:04.400
But by the same token, as
they construct new niches,

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:06.440
these novel niches are solidifying

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:08.090
the differing cultural practices

00:52:08.090 --> 00:52:11.490
by which they were constructed
in the first place, right?

00:52:11.490 --> 00:52:16.490
So you've got from somewhere
between 170,000 years ago,

00:52:16.500 --> 00:52:18.670
you've got the lineage of humans

00:52:18.670 --> 00:52:19.920
that populated the whole world

00:52:19.920 --> 00:52:22.023
that is moving back through Africa,

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:24.730
interbreeding with other hominin,

00:52:24.730 --> 00:52:26.920
other sapient groups
that are there already,

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:30.300
moving out from Africa and
moving across the whole world

00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:32.910
so quickly that that by 65,000 years ago,

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:35.008
they're already in Australia.

00:52:35.008 --> 00:52:36.500
You've got all of this happening

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:39.160
on the basis of the
successive construction

00:52:39.160 --> 00:52:41.240
of niche after niche after niche

00:52:41.240 --> 00:52:45.060
that can only come about through
the the offline imaginary,

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:48.490
the complexities of
epicyclic niche construction

00:52:48.490 --> 00:52:51.500
that enables the discovery of
new resources at every moment,

00:52:51.500 --> 00:52:53.760
that then reshapes the whole nature

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:55.830
of the niche construction
in that circumstance,

00:52:55.830 --> 00:52:58.380
but that also then forms a solidification

00:52:58.380 --> 00:53:00.431
of certain kinds, of a practice,

00:53:00.431 --> 00:53:04.340
a set of unfolding cultural
differences across the world,

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:07.143
given the niches that
are constructed, right?

00:53:09.201 --> 00:53:11.120
And this, one of my favorite maps,

00:53:11.120 --> 00:53:12.390
it's out of date now in many ways,

00:53:12.390 --> 00:53:14.343
but it shows a very simplified picture

00:53:14.343 --> 00:53:16.770
of mitochondrial lineages traced.

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:19.300
Is is the L1 lineage in Africa.

00:53:19.300 --> 00:53:21.160
L1 and L2 moved back into Africa.

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:23.810
L1, see, L3, rather, moved out of Africa.

00:53:23.810 --> 00:53:26.490
The M path was the path that went

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:29.090
along the southern coast of Asia.

00:53:29.090 --> 00:53:30.880
The N path went into Central Asia

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:32.400
and turned back into Europe,

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:35.360
with an R path that is
not even named here,

00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:38.840
and so on and so forth, all
the way to successive waves

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:42.033
that made it all the way to the
tip of South America finally

00:53:42.033 --> 00:53:44.753
between say 70,000 years ago and,

00:53:44.753 --> 00:53:46.370
well the tip of South America,

00:53:46.370 --> 00:53:48.900
probably something on the
order of 15,000 years ago.

00:53:48.900 --> 00:53:52.914
But, the message here is that
the populating of the world

00:53:52.914 --> 00:53:54.840
could only come about by virtue

00:53:54.840 --> 00:53:56.870
of the diversified
cultural niche construction

00:53:56.870 --> 00:54:00.463
that was made available
by systematized semiosis.

00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:03.278
That's it, thank you very much.

00:54:03.278 --> 00:54:06.278
(audience clapping)

00:54:10.768 --> 00:54:12.518
Thank you, thank you.

00:54:17.370 --> 00:54:18.530
- [Priya] That was excellent,
thank you very much.

00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:20.040
Questions?

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:22.100
- We do have a microphone that Ty has.

00:54:22.100 --> 00:54:24.493
I'll ask you to use it if
you want to ask a question.

00:54:26.780 --> 00:54:28.330
Can you pass that down, please?

00:54:31.940 --> 00:54:33.060
- [Audience Member]
Well that was wonderful.

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:33.893
- Thank you very much.

00:54:33.893 --> 00:54:35.313
- [Audience Member] I'm
sure everyone agrees.

00:54:36.730 --> 00:54:39.820
So you take us back 500,000 years

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:44.803
and kind of up to the verge
of modern civilization,

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:50.526
and you have extremely slow development

00:54:50.526 --> 00:54:53.520
during most of that time
compared to what we're used to.

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:55.130
- Right, right.

00:54:55.130 --> 00:54:57.140
- [Audience Member] And
then in the second half

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:01.033
of the last century, you
begin to see this incredible,

00:55:02.385 --> 00:55:07.053
incredible hybridization,
cultural development,

00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:10.493
getting faster and faster year by year,

00:55:11.808 --> 00:55:13.840
and I wonder if you could
comment on that trajectory.

00:55:13.840 --> 00:55:16.320
- Absolutely, absolutely,
yeah it's a great question,

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:18.530
and it is a very real trajectory.

00:55:18.530 --> 00:55:20.265
I would say only one
thing about the trajectory

00:55:20.265 --> 00:55:21.850
as you've laid it out.

00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:23.550
I think the newest archeological evidence

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:25.663
suggests that the
acceleration is happening

00:55:25.663 --> 00:55:27.670
almost as much as 100,000 years ago.

00:55:27.670 --> 00:55:29.195
It is really taking off.

00:55:29.195 --> 00:55:32.150
It is not suggesting that something unique

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:33.940
happened 45,000 years ago in Europe

00:55:33.940 --> 00:55:35.470
with the Upper Paleolithic Revolution.

00:55:35.470 --> 00:55:38.830
That's deeply out, because
so much African evidence

00:55:38.830 --> 00:55:41.617
is coming on board now,
just in the last 25 years,

00:55:41.617 --> 00:55:43.970
it is just remaking the whole picture,

00:55:43.970 --> 00:55:46.543
and the picture now has
very very complicated,

00:55:46.543 --> 00:55:48.880
I would say, I would say the picture now

00:55:48.880 --> 00:55:53.057
has human modernity taking
shape by about 70,000 years ago.

00:55:53.057 --> 00:55:57.123
In other words, Homo sapiens,
at least this L lineage,

00:55:57.123 --> 00:55:59.420
were essentially like us then,

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:00.860
if without all kinds of attainments

00:56:00.860 --> 00:56:03.410
that have come since then, but
they were essentially like us

00:56:03.410 --> 00:56:06.835
and I'm just waiting for the
moment when musical instruments

00:56:06.835 --> 00:56:11.260
30,000 years older than griffon
ulna are found in Africa.

00:56:11.260 --> 00:56:14.340
I'm convinced that they were doing,

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:17.510
given the technologies that
are now recorded for them,

00:56:17.510 --> 00:56:19.357
that they were doing just
immensely complicated things.

00:56:19.357 --> 00:56:21.810
103,000 years ago, Blombos Cave,

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:23.890
they keep digging down and
down and down in this cave

00:56:23.890 --> 00:56:25.440
on the southern African coast,

00:56:25.440 --> 00:56:27.080
the deeper they get the
older they get, of course,

00:56:27.080 --> 00:56:29.300
and 103,000 years ago is
the level to which they came

00:56:29.300 --> 00:56:33.110
a number of years ago, about 10 years ago

00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:35.208
they got to 103,000-year level,

00:56:35.208 --> 00:56:38.100
where they found paint pots, in essence,

00:56:38.100 --> 00:56:41.110
abalone shells that had
dried paint mixtures in,

00:56:41.110 --> 00:56:43.167
with the stirring sticks
there, too, right.

00:56:43.167 --> 00:56:45.760
That's 103,000 years ago,
they're painting their bodies,

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:46.593
they're painting something,

00:56:46.593 --> 00:56:47.850
they're doing all kinds of stuff, right,

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:51.330
so now that sort of
extends your trajectory

00:56:51.330 --> 00:56:53.160
a little bit farther back.

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:55.240
The question of the
acceleration is of course then

00:56:55.240 --> 00:56:57.528
the real kernel of your question,

00:56:57.528 --> 00:56:59.360
and it's a very very interesting thing.

00:56:59.360 --> 00:57:02.849
I think that burgeoning epicycles
explain the acceleration,

00:57:02.849 --> 00:57:04.710
and I've talked about this a lot

00:57:04.710 --> 00:57:06.576
in "Culture and the Course
of Human Evolution."

00:57:06.576 --> 00:57:10.237
The way in which the the
production of epicycles

00:57:10.237 --> 00:57:15.237
could spur not only, it's partly that the,

00:57:16.835 --> 00:57:20.760
what it spurs is a quickening accumulation

00:57:20.760 --> 00:57:23.630
of cultural archives,
as I tend to call them.

00:57:23.630 --> 00:57:26.030
That is to say, of the
sheer amount of material

00:57:26.030 --> 00:57:28.016
that has passed along from
generation to generation,

00:57:28.016 --> 00:57:29.860
that has something to do, obviously,

00:57:29.860 --> 00:57:31.180
with cognitive development

00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:33.000
and all kinds of other things, as well,

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:35.010
and this is hugely complicated to get at,

00:57:35.010 --> 00:57:36.810
but it seems to me it
also has something to do

00:57:36.810 --> 00:57:40.000
with the need in a
deepening cultural archive

00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:41.846
to systematize what is transmitted.

00:57:41.846 --> 00:57:45.010
The function of those systems,
then, in driving forward

00:57:45.010 --> 00:57:47.180
towards new systems and
finally accelerating

00:57:47.180 --> 00:57:49.080
the whole process, so there's a kind of,

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:50.240
I wouldn't call it bootstrapping

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:52.270
'cause that's associated with other views

00:57:52.270 --> 00:57:55.080
of hominin evolution that
I don't quite agree with.

00:57:55.080 --> 00:57:56.800
It's a term of art that is used for them,

00:57:56.800 --> 00:57:58.320
but it is like that.

00:57:58.320 --> 00:58:01.800
It is as if the very
process of the formation

00:58:01.800 --> 00:58:05.152
of these epicycles is one
that is going to accelerate

00:58:05.152 --> 00:58:07.974
the accumulation of more cultural material

00:58:07.974 --> 00:58:10.120
to form more cultural epicycles.

00:58:10.120 --> 00:58:11.210
Something along those lines

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:13.330
is where I'd go with that, yeah, yeah.

00:58:13.330 --> 00:58:14.163
- [Audience Member] Okay.

00:58:14.163 --> 00:58:15.340
- Hello, Gunter.

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:18.000
- [Gunter] So I'm not sure
I understood you correctly,

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:22.010
but I perceived as if you were suggesting

00:58:22.010 --> 00:58:25.982
that cultural differentiation
is a relatively late event

00:58:25.982 --> 00:58:30.700
in your hierarchy, and
I'm surprised about this,

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:34.494
because I think, because this
epicycles that you talk about

00:58:34.494 --> 00:58:37.870
are in a way arbitrary, like they

00:58:37.870 --> 00:58:39.708
are not ecologically necessary,

00:58:39.708 --> 00:58:41.910
and since they are arbitrary,

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:45.110
so cultural differentiation
should actually happen

00:58:45.110 --> 00:58:46.754
as soon as there are epicycles,

00:58:46.754 --> 00:58:50.108
because different epicycles
are likely to arise

00:58:50.108 --> 00:58:52.342
in different populations.

00:58:52.342 --> 00:58:54.073
As soon as they have epicycles,

00:58:54.073 --> 00:58:55.878
then it's cultural differentiation.

00:58:55.878 --> 00:58:57.500
Or did I misunderstand you there?

00:58:57.500 --> 00:59:01.730
- So I've put I put the
burgeoning of cultural difference

00:59:01.730 --> 00:59:04.130
back 70 to 100,000 years, right?

00:59:04.130 --> 00:59:06.995
It's not that recent, first of all, right,

00:59:06.995 --> 00:59:09.850
but that's the first thing,

00:59:09.850 --> 00:59:12.132
so I'm stretching it back a
little bit farther than that,

00:59:12.132 --> 00:59:15.620
so the bead-making epicycle
is a good instance of this,

00:59:15.620 --> 00:59:17.930
that is to say, and it
is an instance of the way

00:59:17.930 --> 00:59:20.860
in which these cultural epicycles
are not in fact arbitrary

00:59:20.860 --> 00:59:22.880
but depend on the
affordance and constraint

00:59:22.880 --> 00:59:24.335
of a particular environment, right.

00:59:24.335 --> 00:59:25.661
There is--
- So it's arbitrary

00:59:25.661 --> 00:59:28.890
whether you start using
a particular foods?

00:59:28.890 --> 00:59:32.310
- It is, again, if you think
of the bead-making example,

00:59:32.310 --> 00:59:33.450
it's not entirely arbitrary.

00:59:33.450 --> 00:59:36.100
If there is a pressure from
growing social complexity

00:59:36.100 --> 00:59:37.730
to mark the complexity somehow,

00:59:37.730 --> 00:59:41.000
certain things are going
to pop up in the cultural,

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:43.930
in the cultural practices of a society

00:59:43.930 --> 00:59:45.860
and other things they're
not going to pop up.

00:59:45.860 --> 00:59:47.179
Those things that do pop up have to do

00:59:47.179 --> 00:59:49.410
with the capabilities of the
humans that are doing them

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:51.290
but they also have to do with
the affordance and constraint

00:59:51.290 --> 00:59:52.940
of the environment,
right, so I don't think

00:59:52.940 --> 00:59:55.260
arbitrariness is the right word

00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:56.691
for the epicycles as a whole,

00:59:56.691 --> 00:59:59.580
but I do take your I do take your point.

00:59:59.580 --> 01:00:01.149
- [Priya] An opportunity, perhaps, right?

01:00:01.149 --> 01:00:04.670
- Yeah, indeed, opportunity
is another word.

01:00:04.670 --> 01:00:06.930
I mean these are, this
is catalyzed, you know,

01:00:06.930 --> 01:00:09.350
to use a word from your neck of the wood

01:00:09.350 --> 01:00:10.650
in molecular biology, right.

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:13.556
This is a catalyzed kind
of cultural development

01:00:13.556 --> 01:00:17.190
that is part and parcel
of the ongoing cycle

01:00:17.190 --> 01:00:21.930
of niche construction, but
your question still remains

01:00:21.930 --> 01:00:24.440
as to, shouldn't difference be popping up

01:00:24.440 --> 01:00:26.450
as soon as these things, and I say,

01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:28.140
yes it should, yes it is.

01:00:28.140 --> 01:00:32.430
So I didn't mean to say
that, what I meant to say

01:00:32.430 --> 01:00:35.214
about recent cultural
difference in Homo sapiens

01:00:35.214 --> 01:00:37.848
is that it has enabled,
it is part and parcel

01:00:37.848 --> 01:00:40.730
of what has enabled the
populating of the whole world.

01:00:40.730 --> 01:00:43.690
On a much more limited scale,
humans in northern Germany

01:00:43.690 --> 01:00:45.536
are making spruce javelins

01:00:45.536 --> 01:00:47.490
because that's what they need,

01:00:47.490 --> 01:00:48.560
that's what they got there,

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:49.960
and that that's what their technology

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:51.770
and their cognition will allow them to do,

01:00:51.770 --> 01:00:54.600
so that is a kind of cultural difference.

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:56.630
The bead making, the
differences in bead materials,

01:00:56.630 --> 01:00:58.830
is a kind of cultural difference
that comes to be fixed

01:00:58.830 --> 01:01:00.100
and solidified in cultures.

01:01:00.100 --> 01:01:01.743
Yeah, I think that's true, yeah.

01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:05.509
- [Audience Member] I was just wondering

01:01:05.509 --> 01:01:07.120
since you're taking questions,

01:01:07.120 --> 01:01:11.343
is there anything that you have
found difficult to explain?

01:01:11.343 --> 01:01:13.361
Something as--
- Almost everything

01:01:13.361 --> 01:01:16.640
that I've, almost everything.

01:01:16.640 --> 01:01:18.250
Well you know, it's a good question,

01:01:18.250 --> 01:01:20.800
but look at this this Homo luzonensis

01:01:20.800 --> 01:01:22.350
announced last week, right.

01:01:22.350 --> 01:01:27.350
Homo luzonensis is, by the
the preliminary announcement

01:01:28.050 --> 01:01:29.690
of this, I mean they've
been studying it for years.

01:01:29.690 --> 01:01:31.357
These fossils were found a while ago

01:01:31.357 --> 01:01:32.878
and they only now released this.

01:01:32.878 --> 01:01:35.360
This is a creature about four feet tall

01:01:35.360 --> 01:01:37.499
that managed to get to the island of Luzon

01:01:37.499 --> 01:01:39.650
and was active there somewhere

01:01:39.650 --> 01:01:41.510
between 70 and 50,000 years ago,

01:01:41.510 --> 01:01:43.160
something on that order, right?

01:01:43.160 --> 01:01:46.350
It is not Homo sapiens, so
it's a different lineage.

01:01:46.350 --> 01:01:49.732
Now a million and 3/4,
1 3/4 million years ago,

01:01:49.732 --> 01:01:52.990
there were earlier waves of
hominins coming out of Africa,

01:01:52.990 --> 01:01:56.520
Homo erectus was one big group in Africa

01:01:56.520 --> 01:01:58.930
that is usually in its Eurasian versions

01:01:58.930 --> 01:02:00.503
called Homo ergaster, right,

01:02:01.763 --> 01:02:06.763
but, so, you know, there was, by the way,

01:02:07.287 --> 01:02:10.103
a second discovery about 20 years ago,

01:02:10.103 --> 01:02:13.130
which was the Homo floresiensis,
the so-called Hobbit

01:02:13.130 --> 01:02:15.387
of an Indonesian island of Flores, right.

01:02:15.387 --> 01:02:17.010
These are unexplained.

01:02:17.010 --> 01:02:20.697
We don't understand how the
the Homo ergaster lineages

01:02:20.697 --> 01:02:22.840
continued to develop.

01:02:22.840 --> 01:02:24.780
We don't understand
what kind of technology

01:02:24.780 --> 01:02:27.690
they might have had
across their evolution.

01:02:27.690 --> 01:02:29.430
There's very very little
hint that they could have had

01:02:29.430 --> 01:02:31.450
seafaring technology, we don't get that.

01:02:31.450 --> 01:02:34.320
We don't understand how,
to go back to Homo sapiens,

01:02:34.320 --> 01:02:36.020
how they moved as quickly as they did

01:02:36.020 --> 01:02:37.230
once they started moving.

01:02:37.230 --> 01:02:40.930
It seems unlikely, and
this is partly judging

01:02:40.930 --> 01:02:43.100
from climate patterns
and so on and so forth,

01:02:43.100 --> 01:02:44.910
when it would have been
easy to get out of Africa

01:02:44.910 --> 01:02:46.030
and when not and so on.

01:02:46.030 --> 01:02:49.100
It seems unlikely that Homo sapiens

01:02:49.100 --> 01:02:50.920
in the final migration out of Africa

01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:52.530
that did populate the whole world,

01:02:52.530 --> 01:02:54.260
there were earlier ones that didn't last,

01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:55.840
into the Levant in places like that,

01:02:55.840 --> 01:02:59.070
but in the final push that
populated the whole world,

01:02:59.070 --> 01:03:02.211
it seems unlikely that began
much before 70,000 years ago.

01:03:02.211 --> 01:03:04.189
But they made it to Australia

01:03:04.189 --> 01:03:06.220
as little as 5,000 years later.

01:03:06.220 --> 01:03:08.290
This is just astonishing,
how does this happen, right?

01:03:08.290 --> 01:03:10.410
And where is the technology
for this to happen,

01:03:10.410 --> 01:03:12.710
and yeah, it's one thing after another,

01:03:12.710 --> 01:03:13.830
one question after another.

01:03:13.830 --> 01:03:18.830
How do you manage to carve a spruce trunk

01:03:19.327 --> 01:03:23.090
at the hardest portion
of wood at its base,

01:03:23.090 --> 01:03:27.420
and create such a lovely
javelin out of stone tools?

01:03:27.420 --> 01:03:28.710
Well clearly you can
do all kinds of things

01:03:28.710 --> 01:03:29.870
with stone tools, we know that.

01:03:29.870 --> 01:03:33.040
But these are not easy feats, right,

01:03:33.040 --> 01:03:35.750
so it's, it is all of it astonishing.

01:03:35.750 --> 01:03:37.240
The achievements of these early humans,

01:03:37.240 --> 01:03:39.063
not only sapiens but Neanderthals,

01:03:40.662 --> 01:03:43.850
the Denisovans, another lineage
that was only discovered

01:03:43.850 --> 01:03:46.003
in the 1990s from southern Siberia.

01:03:46.003 --> 01:03:51.003
We have, not all of us, but we
have bits of Denisovan genes

01:03:51.103 --> 01:03:52.730
in our gene pool.

01:03:52.730 --> 01:03:55.150
There was some interbreeding
between sapiens and Denisovans.

01:03:55.150 --> 01:03:56.320
There was a good deal of interbreeding

01:03:56.320 --> 01:03:58.529
between sapiens and
Neanderthals, we now know.

01:03:58.529 --> 01:04:00.943
But the movements of these things,

01:04:00.943 --> 01:04:02.820
the movements of these populations,

01:04:02.820 --> 01:04:04.330
there were waves and
waves and waves of them

01:04:04.330 --> 01:04:05.460
that we don't know about,

01:04:05.460 --> 01:04:07.243
that we have only the
slightest glimmerings of

01:04:07.243 --> 01:04:09.673
in the technological record.

01:04:10.980 --> 01:04:14.340
It's all extraordinary, it's
an extraordinary history.

01:04:14.340 --> 01:04:15.980
To me it's just absolutely fascinating,

01:04:15.980 --> 01:04:18.700
continues to be fascinating,
and my effort, then,

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:22.920
is to propose a model that
helps us to understand

01:04:24.020 --> 01:04:28.579
how some of this mobility,
some of this differentiation,

01:04:28.579 --> 01:04:30.740
how all of it could come about

01:04:30.740 --> 01:04:33.440
that is not requiring some miracle gene

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:35.710
that makes us linguistic
creatures, for example,

01:04:35.710 --> 01:04:37.420
but rather has a different set

01:04:37.420 --> 01:04:39.600
of biocultural interdependencies

01:04:39.600 --> 01:04:42.200
that is what spells the changes

01:04:42.200 --> 01:04:44.033
over several hundred thousand years.

01:04:45.640 --> 01:04:47.243
I don't know whether that
helps you very much, but--

01:04:47.243 --> 01:04:49.571
- [Audience Member] Yeah and
to get back to the second

01:04:49.571 --> 01:04:50.404
question also, since--

01:04:50.404 --> 01:04:52.290
- Hold the mic up
further, hold the mic up.

01:04:52.290 --> 01:04:55.560
- [Audience Member] What is,
you talk about in this culture

01:04:55.560 --> 01:04:58.495
is something that belongs to humans,

01:04:58.495 --> 01:05:00.871
by the nature of seniority.

01:05:00.871 --> 01:05:05.010
But you know, I remember
watching this feature

01:05:05.010 --> 01:05:09.320
on PBS about cuttlefish, which turned out

01:05:09.320 --> 01:05:11.567
to be more sophisticated than I thought,

01:05:11.567 --> 01:05:13.270
and the behavior, the mating behavior

01:05:13.270 --> 01:05:15.450
and everything else, in offering to it

01:05:16.940 --> 01:05:18.607
was amazingly human.

01:05:19.985 --> 01:05:23.485
So how you do you decide what is cultural?

01:05:24.345 --> 01:05:26.370
- Yeah, so it's a very
good very good question.

01:05:26.370 --> 01:05:27.380
The first thing to say is that

01:05:27.380 --> 01:05:29.420
I didn't restrict culture to humans.

01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:30.910
I think whole culture is widespread

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:33.430
through a large group of
animals in the world today.

01:05:33.430 --> 01:05:36.410
However, there's a difference
between social organization

01:05:36.410 --> 01:05:39.130
and complexity in ants
and social organization

01:05:39.130 --> 01:05:41.490
and complexity say in
a group of chimpanzees.

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:42.860
And the difference has everything to do

01:05:42.860 --> 01:05:45.270
with the learning of information
that you then transmit

01:05:45.270 --> 01:05:46.330
to the next generation.

01:05:46.330 --> 01:05:47.998
Ants don't do that, right?

01:05:47.998 --> 01:05:51.920
They are genetically
programmed to do certain things

01:05:51.920 --> 01:05:53.787
and interact with one
another in certain ways.

01:05:53.787 --> 01:05:55.915
I think cuttlefish, which are fascinating,

01:05:55.915 --> 01:05:58.700
cuttlefish have, you did
mention cuttlefish, didn't you?

01:05:58.700 --> 01:05:59.800
- Yes.
- Yeah, cuttlefish

01:05:59.800 --> 01:06:03.370
have as many as 30 to 35
different flashing signs.

01:06:03.370 --> 01:06:06.480
I once saw a film, an
amazing film, of somebody

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:09.690
who in a laboratory held an
American flag up in front

01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:12.105
of the thing and honest to god,

01:06:12.105 --> 01:06:15.250
it flashed red white and blue
and it's just amazing, yeah.

01:06:15.250 --> 01:06:18.147
So they had these extraordinary
semiotic capacities

01:06:18.147 --> 01:06:22.370
and the chromatophores and
those are hooked up to neurons

01:06:22.370 --> 01:06:23.779
in all kinds of complicated ways.

01:06:23.779 --> 01:06:26.890
I never want to underplay the complexity

01:06:26.890 --> 01:06:29.440
of cephalopods, octopuses are amazing,

01:06:29.440 --> 01:06:32.040
squids are amazing,
cuttlefish are amazing.

01:06:32.040 --> 01:06:35.270
Yeah, I don't know that
we have any evidence

01:06:35.270 --> 01:06:38.590
that octopuses, squid and
cuttlefish are cultural animals

01:06:38.590 --> 01:06:40.810
according to the the outlines

01:06:40.810 --> 01:06:44.208
that I've been talking about
here, but we do have evidence

01:06:44.208 --> 01:06:46.510
that they do very very complicated things.

01:06:46.510 --> 01:06:48.490
The latest report I saw was an octopus

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:50.640
using a shell as a tool.

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:53.400
Tool-using octopuses, who'd a thunk,

01:06:53.400 --> 01:06:56.433
but it seems, it's
extraordinary, yeah, yeah.

01:06:58.180 --> 01:06:59.620
I will say one more thing about this,

01:06:59.620 --> 01:07:02.760
in answer to this question, the
one thing I never want to do

01:07:02.760 --> 01:07:06.910
in trying to, I don't think
one has to de-emphasize

01:07:06.910 --> 01:07:09.820
the complexity of other
animals in order to emphasize

01:07:09.820 --> 01:07:11.130
the complexity of humans.

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:13.510
That's simply not a dichotomy
that is useful to me.

01:07:13.510 --> 01:07:15.928
Other animals do amazing things.

01:07:15.928 --> 01:07:20.928
Ask Rick, he knows, yeah
go, ahead, Francesco.

01:07:21.147 --> 01:07:23.393
- [Francesco] Thank you so much.

01:07:24.890 --> 01:07:28.080
Beside some technical
question we can discuss

01:07:28.080 --> 01:07:30.820
during the party, there are two point.

01:07:30.820 --> 01:07:33.780
One point that is important to me.

01:07:33.780 --> 01:07:38.780
In this wonderful depiction you gave us,

01:07:39.168 --> 01:07:43.810
some of the passages
imply at least two things

01:07:44.811 --> 01:07:48.380
that are worthy of mentioning.

01:07:48.380 --> 01:07:51.800
First there are passages that serve

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:56.800
or look at different and opposite goals,

01:07:57.800 --> 01:08:02.290
for example the passage on
from in hyper-indexicality

01:08:02.290 --> 01:08:07.290
to offline is instrumental
both to the construction

01:08:07.430 --> 01:08:12.023
of an archive and the
construction of an imagination.

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:18.190
And the second is every
passage implies also losses.

01:08:19.280 --> 01:08:20.810
- I didn't hear that, means?

01:08:20.810 --> 01:08:22.830
- [Francesco] You lose something.

01:08:22.830 --> 01:08:27.830
For example, to go offline,
it's a dramatic moment

01:08:27.980 --> 01:08:30.963
in which you lose
contact with the reality.

01:08:32.210 --> 01:08:37.210
Reality goes away and you
need to say goodbye to reality

01:08:37.750 --> 01:08:42.060
in order to perform a
number of semiotic acts.

01:08:42.060 --> 01:08:43.747
And this is a very dramatic moment

01:08:43.747 --> 01:08:48.220
in which you are no longer
tuned with the reality,

01:08:48.220 --> 01:08:52.350
and you have to refurbish or re-launch

01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:54.620
the circle of the entire semiosis

01:08:54.620 --> 01:08:57.460
in order to fulfill this lack.

01:08:57.460 --> 01:09:00.957
- Yeah it's tremendously
dramatic, Francesco, I agree,

01:09:00.957 --> 01:09:04.780
but I don't necessarily agree
that you in fact have to lose

01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:07.840
reality in order to get
this expanded imaginary.

01:09:07.840 --> 01:09:12.840
So what is happening with
humans 40,000 years ago

01:09:13.400 --> 01:09:15.714
painting a mammoth on the cave wall,

01:09:15.714 --> 01:09:18.527
there is an exercise of
the offline imagination,

01:09:18.527 --> 01:09:20.710
and it's clear that they're
not hunting the mammoth

01:09:20.710 --> 01:09:22.140
at that moment when
they're painting it, right,

01:09:22.140 --> 01:09:24.580
so maybe that counts as a loss, however,

01:09:24.580 --> 01:09:28.471
what they are doing is, I see
it more as an expanded reality

01:09:28.471 --> 01:09:31.330
rather than a loss of
touch with reality, right.

01:09:31.330 --> 01:09:33.180
What they are doing is
expanding their reality

01:09:33.180 --> 01:09:34.830
so that something is brought into the cave

01:09:34.830 --> 01:09:36.190
with a presence that it hadn't had

01:09:36.190 --> 01:09:38.566
in the cave before, all right, and that,

01:09:38.566 --> 01:09:42.523
so I would only disagree
with the question,

01:09:42.523 --> 01:09:45.861
with the vocabulary of loss
that is at that moment.

01:09:45.861 --> 01:09:47.300
- [Francesco] They killed the animals.

01:09:47.300 --> 01:09:49.188
- They?
- They killed the animals.

01:09:49.188 --> 01:09:50.021
- Well, sure.

01:09:50.021 --> 01:09:53.010
- [Francesco] Yeah, and
the animals disappeared.

01:09:53.010 --> 01:09:54.060
(Gary laughs)

01:09:54.060 --> 01:09:56.481
So there's this what we call loss,

01:09:56.481 --> 01:09:57.810
and of restoration.

01:09:57.810 --> 01:10:00.640
- Well when they were painting
these animals on the walls

01:10:00.640 --> 01:10:02.270
they hadn't killed them all off, right?

01:10:02.270 --> 01:10:04.860
There were plenty of woolly
mammoths outside the cave

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:07.320
when they were painting
these pictures, right?

01:10:07.320 --> 01:10:08.599
We know that's that for
a fact, I'm not saying,

01:10:08.599 --> 01:10:11.453
you don't have to agree,
it is simply true, right,

01:10:11.453 --> 01:10:16.453
so yes, but these were animals
that these humans hunted.

01:10:16.667 --> 01:10:18.860
They were animals that
they interacted with

01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:21.750
in all the complex ways
that such complex parts

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:23.895
of niche construction would
interact with one another,

01:10:23.895 --> 01:10:27.100
to expand the horizon of
that niche construction

01:10:27.100 --> 01:10:28.730
so that they are brought into the cave

01:10:28.730 --> 01:10:30.460
is very very dramatic, as you say.

01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:33.541
Yeah, yeah, absolutely
dramatic and suggests,

01:10:33.541 --> 01:10:36.270
well, it suggests, among other things,

01:10:36.270 --> 01:10:38.220
it suggests to me the double indexicality

01:10:38.220 --> 01:10:39.660
of ritual at work, right,

01:10:39.660 --> 01:10:41.184
to suggest something like that, yeah.

01:10:41.184 --> 01:10:42.400
- [Priya] So I have a very quick question

01:10:42.400 --> 01:10:47.150
before we close, so where
does intentionality fit in?

01:10:47.150 --> 01:10:49.090
- Oh it's right at the heart of it, okay.

01:10:49.090 --> 01:10:51.513
I mean, by which you mean the
philosophers intentionality,

01:10:51.513 --> 01:10:52.346
- Intentionality, yes,
- Not my intent

01:10:52.346 --> 01:10:54.580
to do something, yeah, indeed.

01:10:54.580 --> 01:10:57.780
Intentionality is, in fact, a
question of aboutness, right.

01:10:57.780 --> 01:11:00.230
It is about a mind directed to something.

01:11:00.230 --> 01:11:03.240
My notion of semiosis is about minds

01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:05.490
that are directed in
that notion of attention

01:11:05.490 --> 01:11:07.080
as opposed to intention.
- The intention.

01:11:07.080 --> 01:11:08.860
- But it is, it seems to me, attention

01:11:08.860 --> 01:11:11.280
that is going to be a
foundation for intentionality.

01:11:11.280 --> 01:11:14.120
So intentionality, then, is
a useful philosopher's term,

01:11:14.120 --> 01:11:15.400
it's a very important one, obviously,

01:11:15.400 --> 01:11:17.210
lots of philosophers talk about it,

01:11:17.210 --> 01:11:21.554
but it is one that is fixed
on human states of mind,

01:11:21.554 --> 01:11:24.094
and the reason that I don't use the term--

01:11:24.094 --> 01:11:25.363
- They got it somewhere.
- Is precisely to

01:11:25.363 --> 01:11:28.051
get away from that, yeah, yeah yeah, yeah.

01:11:28.051 --> 01:11:29.710
But it is related, yeah, yeah.

01:11:29.710 --> 01:11:31.102
- [Priya] So Gary, thanks
so much for your speech.

01:11:31.102 --> 01:11:31.935
- Thank you all.
(audience clapping)

01:11:31.935 --> 01:11:35.059
Thank you very much, thank you.

01:11:35.059 --> 01:11:35.892
Thank you.

01:11:38.872 --> 01:11:41.455
(gentle music)

